## 📌 5. Wrapper Classes — Primitive → Object

> Every primitive type has a **Wrapper Class** that converts it into an object.

| Primitive | Wrapper Class |
|---|---|
| `int` | `Integer` |
| `double` | `Double` |
| `char` | `Character` |
| `boolean` | `Boolean` |
| `float` | `Float` |
| `long` | `Long` |

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // int → Integer (Autoboxing)
        Integer a = 10;

        // Integer → int (Unboxing)
        int b = a;

        // Useful methods
        System.out.println(Integer.MAX_VALUE);         // 2147483647
        System.out.println(Integer.MIN_VALUE);         // -2147483648
        System.out.println(Integer.parseInt("42"));    // String → int
        System.out.println(Double.parseDouble("3.14")); // String → double
        System.out.println(Integer.toBinaryString(10)); // 1010
        System.out.println(Integer.toHexString(255));   // ff
    }
}
```

### 🔄 Why use Wrapper Classes?
```java
// Primitives can't be used in Collections — Wrapper classes can!
ArrayList<Integer> list = new ArrayList<>();  // ✅
ArrayList<int> list = new ArrayList<>();      // ❌ won't work
```

